WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:16.550 align:start position:0%
 
viewed from a rooftop  filmed by a terrified witness an attack  that traumatized and changed a nation  after the murders at the headquarters of  magazine charlie hebdo  france became a different more nervous  country facing  fundamental questions about free speech  and religion  a verdict has been reached but the  effects linger to this day  this is still a quiet street but now it  carries  memories of the victims of a bleak and  brutal morning  [Music]  january 2015 two heavily armed men  attacked the offices of charlie hebdo  many of the staff had received death  threats for years  after publishing caricatures of the  prophet muhammad  within minutes 12 people are dead  including eight journalists  and two police officers  the man shouts out the prophet muhammad  has been avenged  we have killed charlie hebdo and then  the pair drive off  this man was the first doctor on the  scene he'd written for charlie hebdo  among the victims were some of his close  friends  the attack that took place inside this  building shocked france not just because  of its  callous brutality but also because  charlie hebdo was only targeted because  the magazine  had chosen to publish some cartoons that  some people  found deeply offensive and the  repercussions of this attack  and those cartoons continue to be felt  the faces of the charlie hebdo  cartoonists look out from this mural  among them george wallinsky who was 80  years old when he was shot  we went to talk to his widow even now  long after her husband's murder  she lives with 24-hour police protection  [Music]  she says that despite the pain it's  caused charlie hebdo  was right to publish the caricatures  [Music]  of  these were the killers brothers syed and  sheriff kawachi  themselves killed by police a couple of  days later after a manhunt that finished  on the outskirts of paris  as that unfolded another attack was  launched on a jewish  supermarket in the east of the capital  by a close friend of the kochie brothers  ahmedi kulabali who had also pledged  allegiance to the islamic state  took several hostages and killed four  people  the siege was ended when heavily armed  police stormed the building  shooting and killing coulee valley  this series of attacks prompted a year  of heightened alert across france  and the worst fears were realized in  november 2015  when 130 people were killed in one  terrible evening in paris at the  bataclan concert hall  and in nearby bars it was the worst  attack in france  since the second world war the country  entered a state of emergency  more than five years on and finally in  this paris courtroom  a trial of those suspected of being  accomplices  to the grim attacks in january 2015  men and women accused of helping the  three attackers plan  and execute the murders of so many  people  at the start of the trial charlie hebdo  made the controversial  provocative decision to republish those  caricatures  a move that once again brought a return  to violent attacks  returning france to its highest level of  terror alert  an echo of what happened in 2015.  the karachi brothers came up the stairs  in this building that went through  these double doors and into the offices  of charlie hebdo  where they carried out the attack that  seemed to change  france but that wasn't the end of the  story for this building  five years later it was targeted once  again  two people from this office were stabbed  as they smoked a cigarette outside  once again the police swarmed the area  both victims survived but with  serious long-term injuries the attacker  thought they were workers from charlie  hebdo in fact the magazine had moved  years before not long after came the  beheading  of teacher samuel patty in the paris  suburbs and then  a triple murder inside the basilica in  nice  both have been linked to anger caused by  the caricatures  the french government though unwavering  in its support of  free speech and the legal right to  [Music]  blaspheme  his words were derided as posturing by  some majority muslim countries  in bangladesh this huge anti-french  protest  turkey's president accused president  macron of being  mentally unfit france is home to six  million muslims  more than any other european nation and  the tension is  clear this paris mosque was closed due  to alleged links with radicalization  but worshipers here and across the  capital have insisted they reject  violence  among the many muslim clerics to condemn  the attack was kemadu gassama  an imam at one of the city's mosques  france remains a nervous country where  people cherish freedom of expression  and talk of terrorism as if it's a war  without end  time has passed but the nerves remain  the scars of charlie hebdo haven't  healed  adam parsons sky news paris

